.TH SDL_realloc 3 "SDL 3.5.0" "Simple Directmedia Layer" "SDL3 FUNCTIONS"
.SH NAME
SDL_realloc \- Change the size of allocated memory.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <SDL3/SDL_stdinc.h>
.PP
.BI "void * SDL_realloc(void *mem, size_t size);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The memory returned by this function must be freed with
.BR SDL_free ()\[char46]
.PP
If
.BR size
is 0, it will be set to 1\[char46] Note that this is unlike some other C runtime
.BR realloc
implementations, which may treat
.BR "realloc(mem, 0)"
the same way as
.BR free(mem) \[char46]
.PP
If
.BR mem
is NULL, the behavior of this function is equivalent to
.BR SDL_malloc ()\[char46]
Otherwise, the function can have one of three possible outcomes:
.PP

\(bu If it returns the same pointer as
.BR mem ,
it means that
.BR mem
was resized in place without freeing\[char46]
.PP
\(bu If it returns a different non-NULL pointer, it means that
.BR mem
was freed and cannot be dereferenced anymore\[char46]
.PP
\(bu If it returns NULL (indicating failure), then
.BR mem
will remain valid and must still be freed with
.BR SDL_free ()\[char46]
.PP
If the allocation is successfully resized, the returned pointer is guaranteed to be aligned to either the
.IR "fundamental alignment"
.BR "" ( alignof(max_align_t)
in C11 and later) or
.BR "2
.IR " sizeof(void " )"
,
whichever is smaller\[char46]

.SH FUNCTION PARAMETERS
.TP
.I mem
a pointer to allocated memory to reallocate, or NULL\[char46]
.TP
.I size
the new size of the memory\[char46]
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns a pointer to the newly allocated memory, or NULL if allocation failed\[char46]

.SH THREAD SAFETY
It is safe to call this function from any thread\[char46]

.SH AVAILABILITY
This function is available since SDL 3\[char46]2\[char46]0\[char46]

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR SDL_free (3),
.BR SDL_malloc (3),
.BR SDL_calloc (3)
